From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Narrow a serving process and take its limits from each Worker

Coworkerd v49, the workerd side of the process sandbox (docs/process-isolation.md sections 27-29) and of the
per-Worker limits of v47. Depends on the CPU limit patch (v29) and the memory limit patch (v33). Fixed
upstream v1.20261012.1. Upstream status: Coworkerd-specific; not submitted.

Two things only the process itself can do, both at its first request -- the node's readiness probe, before it
has answered anything -- in coworkerd-steady.{h,c++}, with one call from Server::WorkerService::startRequest:

  * the steady-state system call filter. The launcher (v44, v45) has to execve workerd, so its own table must
    hold execve and everything else only the startup needs, and a seccomp filter can only ever be narrowed,
    never replaced: the narrower table of the same build (coworkerd-syscalls-steady.txt, 40 of the startup
    table's 62 calls on x86_64) can only go on from in here. COWORKERD_SECCOMP_STEADY_MODE picks off, observe
    or enforce, as for the startup table, and COWORKERD_SECCOMP_STEADY_FILE is the file, which the node binds
    into the process's mount namespace. The layout is the launcher's, so both tables are the same program
    shape. In observe mode the process answers its own notifications -- by then it has no channel to the node
    that could carry a notification listener -- from a thread that exists before the filter does and makes
    only calls the table holds (ioctl, write; the kernel's receive blocks, so it needs no poll, which a
    serving process no longer has), which is why production runs enforce, with no thread at all. Either way
    one [coworkerd-seccomp] row says what was installed;
  * PR_SET_DUMPABLE 0, which the launcher sets before the execve and the kernel then undoes for a readable,
    same-owner binary (v44's ruling: the state endpoint reported the truth, and this is the fix).

The limits of a dynamic Worker are now that Worker's own where it names them (v47 stores them per Worker; the
node's runtime bridge puts them in the loader key, so an isolate is created under its Worker's limits and a
change of them loads another isolate): the isolate name's last segment `limits=<cpuMs>:<memoryMiB>` is read
in coworkerd-cpu-limit, and a limit it does not name stays the process's (COWORKERD_CPU_LIMIT_MS,
COWORKERD_MEMORY_LIMIT_MIB), which is the behaviour before this patch. Every limit event row already carries
the limit it was measured against, so the node reads them per Worker. The memory execution identity (v33)
now follows the isolate's own memory limit as well as the process's, so an isolate limited on its own keeps
reporting who was executing when it died.

